---
import SiteFooter from "@/components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteHeader from "@/components/SiteHeader.astro";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "@/config/site";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { visiblePosts } from "@/lib/posts";

const allPosts = visiblePosts(await getCollection("posts"));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${siteConfig.name} — what it publishes and who writes it.`}
>
  <SiteHeader posts={allPosts} />
  <main id="content" class="outer">
    <div class="canvas">
      <header class="pagehead">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1 class="pagehead-title">{siteConfig.name}</h1>
        <p class="pagehead-description">{siteConfig.about}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What gets published here</h2>
        <p>
          Senior developer at <a href="https://security.gallagher.com/">Gallagher Security</a> by day,
          curious web tinkerer by night.
        </p>

        <h2>Dad</h2>
        <p>Dad to three boys, which means my debugging skills extend well beyond code.</p>

        <h2>Drongo</h2>
        <p><em>drongo</em> | ˈdrɒŋɡəʊ | noun</p>
        <ol>
          <li>A songbird with glossy black plumage and typically a long forked tail and a crest, found in Africa, southern Asia, and Australia.</li>
          <li><em>Australian / New Zealand informal.</em> A stupid or incompetent person.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Origin</h3>
        <p>
          As well as being the name of a bird found in north-eastern Australia, Drongo was the name
          of a racehorse foaled in 1921. It usually performed poorly, although it was placed a number
          of times, and retired in 1925. Thereafter, anyone slow-witted or clumsy was referred to as a
          drongo.
        </p>
        <p>
          The horse was lampooned by cartoonist Sam Wells in drawings depicting “Drongo Mk. 1” in the
          <em> Melbourne Herald</em> during the early 1920s. The word became nationally used as a reference
          to <a href="https://thepirateshipquintet.bandcamp.com/album/rope-for-no-hopers/">no-hopers</a>
          during World War II.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          <p>Drongo wasn’t such a drongo<br />But of course the legend grew<br />And legends are stronger than truth<br />So everyone believes now<br />That Drongo was an old camel<br />Who always ran last.</p>
          <p>But some of us remember a colt<br />Running second in a Derby;<br />He didn’t look so clumsy<br />Just for that second…</p>
          <p>Sure he never won a race,<br />Neither did I, mate;<br />Most of us never win races<br />And so they call us drongoes.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>Wannan, Bill (1979) [1970]. <em>Australian Folklore</em>. Lansdowne Press, p. 200.</p>
        <p>
          “Drongo” went from a racehorse that never won to Aussie slang for a loser — yet its story
          proves that coming second still beats never running at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/media/documents/2025/08/news-article_CAREER-OF-DRONGO.pdf">Read the newspaper clipping: Career of Drongo</a>
        </p>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, corrections, and requests are welcome through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
        </p>
        {
          siteConfig.email && (
            <p>You can also email <a href={`mailto:${siteConfig.email}`}>{siteConfig.email}</a> directly.</p>
          )
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
